{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[0,6],"question":"What real-world application has the distributed word2vec training system been deployed for at Yahoo, and since when?","answer":"Since late 2015, it has been used for matching queries to ads as part of the 'broad' ad matching pipeline in Gemini, Yahoo's sponsored search advertising platform."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two main drawbacks of existing word2vec training systems for large vocabularies?","answer":"They either require that vectors for all vocabulary words be stored in a single server's memory, or they suffer unacceptable training latency due to massive network data transfer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many vectors does each vocabulary word have in word2vec, and how is each one stored?","answer":"Each vocabulary word has two associated k-dimensional vectors, called input and output vectors, each represented as an array of single precision floating point numbers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How much memory would be needed to train vectors for the 200-million-word search advertisement use case described in the paper?","answer":"It would require about 480GB of memory, which is well beyond the capacity of typical commodity servers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How large are the training corpuses used in the search advertising application discussed in the paper?","answer":"The training corpuses are on the order of 30 to 90 billion generalized words."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0056","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What technologies were used to implement the distributed word2vec training system?","answer":"It was implemented in Java and Scala, using Apache Slider and Apache Spark running on a Hadoop YARN-scheduled cluster."}
